X2. Impact of C's Bound on G's M1 and the Exact Surviving Counting Statement

Yes: C's rescued height bound KILLS G's M1 as a small-generator-height (c = 0) mechanism. Because C's theorem forces H >= Omega(m*x/n) ~ x^(3/4), the only surviving escape in linked modules is sparse/anomalous small-norm excess inside a high-height module (where H5_r fails in {-1, 0, 1}^r and V_F = infinity). Furthermore, C's envelope remark (consultant_c.txt:31) kills single-cluster cancellation: if m k-independent candidates had |F(a^(j))| <= Y and shared one O_k^*-rescaled log-conjugate profile box of total width sum_i w_i <= x^(1/2+o(1)), treating those m candidates as generators would violate C's theorem. Any survivor must therefore scatter across many proper k-subspaces or wildly incomparable archimedean profiles.

Precise barrier statement (LP_exc^rel) whose failure is required for alpha < 1/3:
For a reduced linked O_k-module M of relative rank m and degree D = 2*e*n with candidate set Omega subseteq Z^r (|Omega| = e^C), let N_ind(M, Omega; Y) be the maximum number of O_k^*-inequivalent candidates a in Omega with 0 < |F(a)| <= Y whose two-sided prime-ideal parity rows are F_2-independent modulo the span of all candidates lying in proper k-subspaces of M and all multiplicative products of lower-norm candidates. Sub-L[1/3] via linked modules requires LP_exc^rel to FAIL: there must exist an L_N[alpha]-time constructible sequence (M_N, Omega_N) (alpha < 1/3, C <= O(F_alpha)) and Y with log Y <= c_0 * F_{2*alpha} such that N_ind(M_N, Omega_N; Y) * rho(log Y / log B) >= pi(B) for B = L_N[alpha, beta] — i.e., N_ind(M_N, Omega_N; Y) >= exp(Omega(F_alpha)) at log Y = o(x^(2/3)), despite generator envelope H >= Omega(m*x/n).

